./.agent-runner/runs/session-00000000000001790956497071062138-527508-0.log:343:tool stdout: {"content":"# Next prompt ideas\n\nWhen things become complicated, remember the principle of divide and conquer.\n\n---\n\nIn the `status` command output, the `Action:` field value is not aligned with\nthe start position of the values for the other fields.\n\n\nIn the `status` we currently have this entry, where you can see the `action` issue above. However there are other issues too. The reason seems to be truncated, which is not nice. Neither is the reason, which does not seem to be caused by the task, but is rather a limitation in the agent executor. Therefore it seems unreasonable to mark it as `blocked` because the agent execution failed. Wouldn't it be more reasonable to leave it as in-progress or something instead?\n```\n  Component: sandbox_runner\n    State:     blocked\n    Documents: all valid (5/5)\n    Tasks:     4/6 completed (66%) [1 pending, 1 blocked]\n    Action: Resolve the blocked tasks below; 'kvist help task-states' explains each state and its resolution.\n    Blocked:   sandbox-runner-reverification-security-audit\n               reason: agent execution exceeded the combined output limit while contacting the local model\n               gateway.…\n               next:   kvist task run sandbox_runner sandbox-runner-reverification-security-audit\n```\n\n---\n\nIn the `agent-runner` output, I'd like to have color-coded background for different type of content, to differentiate it a bit more.\n\n---\n\nWhat can we learn or get inspired by from either of [Goose](https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose) or [OpenCode](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode), which have at partial Rust implementations. I guess we cannot use either directly since we aim for a more secure wrapping of the agent execution. However, surely there are things that we can pick up from them and be inspired by. They may have much better handling than we do in some areas. Perhaps they have other functionality or features that we could benefit from too. Or perhaps there are parts that we can adopt wholesale? Do an in-depth comparison between these alternatives. Then write down your findings in a Markdown document. Please investigate, ponder and advise. Thanks\n\nHere's what Gemini had to say about it:\n```\n1. Concrete Things to Import from the Rust Ecosystem\n\nRather than reinventing common agentic sub-components, several battle-tested Rust crates and designs from tools like Goose (aaif-goose/goose), Aider, and the broader Rust terminal ecosystem can be integrated directly:\nA. Structured Tool Calling & MCP Engine (goose core or rmcp)\n\n    What it is: Goose's Rust workspace (goose, goose-mcp) implements the Model Context Protocol (MCP) using tokio and serde_json.   \n    Agent Safehouse\n\n    Relevance for Kvist: You can adopt Goose's MCP serialization logic or the official community crates (rmcp / standard Rust MCP servers).\n\n    Sandbox benefit: Instead of giving the LLM raw bash access inside the sandbox for everything, run an MCP proxy on the host that serializes requests and forwards strictly validated JSON-RPC payloads over standard I/O (or a Unix domain socket) into the sandbox container/jail.\n\nB. Syntactical Context Extraction via Tree-Sitter (tree-sitter)\n\n    What it is: Aider’s defining feature is its Repository Map—it parses ASTs to extract function signatures, struct definitions, and import graphs without consuming full token budgets.\n\n    Relevance for Kvist: In Rust, you can use the official tree-sitter and tree-sitter-tags crates (similar to how editors like Zed or tools like ast-grep do it).\n\n    Why it matters: Generating AST maps outside the sandbox on the host or inside a lightweight caching thread allows Kvist to construct token-dense architectural summaries for the LLM before any execution directive starts.\n\nC. In-Memory Diffing and Patch Application (similar / imara-diff)\n\n    What it is: Agents frequently output unified diffs or search-and-replace blocks rather than rewriting whole files.\n\n    Relevance for Kvist: similar (from Armin Ronacher) is arguably the cleanest, zero-dependency Rust crate for computing diffs, unified patch generation, and hunk matching","next_offset":4096,"offset":0,"sha256":"sha256:1c3a4c197a5b3f9b966890b8e0e70449ed8ba93fb9c186275e5afd5c1e67f197","total_bytes":9834}
